Q. @SpringBootApplication
Ans:
 It's  typically placed on your main class to bootstrap a spring Boot application
It includes:
1. @SpringBootConfiguration
- A specialized version of @Configuration
- Indicates that the class can define Spring beans using @Bean
- Marks it as a source if bean definitions.
- It is used with root class means it tells that the class with main method can contain bean.

Note:
- customized behavior: @SpringBootApplication(scanBasePackage = "com.examleproject")
This overrides default component scanning package	
- or it also can exclude specific auto-configuration

2. @EnableAutoConfiguration
- Tells Spring Boot to automatically configure your application based on
	- Dependencies in the classpath
	- Existing beans
	- Application Properties 	
Example:
	If you include a web dependency, it auto-configures an embedded server like Apache Tomcat.

3. @ComponentScan
- Enables component scanning in the package where your main class resides
- Automatically detects: 
	@Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller
- Also scans sub-packages by default 
